{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YWSBWmBhp8wTQJ58cQvNr7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AutoOpt Log\n2\t\n3\t## 2026-04-10-1933-defer-round0-d2h-sync\n4\t\n5\t**Idea**: Defer D2H synchronization in Round 0 by splitting the per-AIR loop into a GPU launch phase and a CPU processing phase, eliminating ~1,246 blocking sync points.\n6\t\n7\t**Result**: success — STARK excl trace APC 300: -104ms vs baseline (2478ms -> 2374ms, 1.04x lower); Round 0 APC 300: -94ms vs baseline (663ms -> 569ms, 1.17x lower)\n8\t\n9\t**Summary**: Restructured `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` to launch all GPU kernels before processing any results. The improvement scales with APC count as expected (APC 0: negligible, APC 100: -86ms Round 0, APC 300: -93ms Round 0). The absolute savings were lower than the plan's estimate of 200-300ms, likely because CPU-side work in the logup kernel setup already provided partial GPU overlap in the original code. The optimization establishes a clean two-phase structure that enables future rayon parallelization of Phase 2 CPU work.\n10\t\n11\t## 2026-04-10-2145-batch-degenerate-stacked-reduction\n12\t\n13\t**Idea**: Batch all degenerate-window kernel launches in Stacked Reduction into a single GPU kernel launch per round, eliminating ~10,348 tiny kernel launches at APC 300.\n14\t\n15\t**Result**: success — STARK excl trace APC 300: -283ms vs baseline (2478ms -> 2195ms, 1.13x lower); Stacked Reduction APC 300: -161ms vs baseline (308ms -> 147ms, 2.10x lower) (vs previous: -165ms, 312ms -> 147ms, 2.12x lower)\n16\t\n17\t**Summary**: Added a batched CUDA kernel where each block handles one degenerate window, all atomically accumulating into a shared output buffer. This replaced ~10,348 individual kernel launches (each with fill_zero + H2D + kernel + D2H sync) with a single launch cycle. The STARK excl trace APC 300/APC 0 scaling ratio improved from 1.153 (baseline) to 1.022, achieving near-parity. Combined with the previous Round 0 optimization, cumulative STARK excl trace improvement at APC 300 is 283ms (11.4%).\n18\t\n19\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7092ff3-dad3-4115-bd08-06aa847648b3","uuid":"88d37527-caa0-458e-acd8-a41f2e3955bd","timestamp":"2026-04-11T07:56:03.756Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/log.md","content":"# AutoOpt Log\n\n## 2026-04-10-1933-defer-round0-d2h-sync\n\n**Idea**: Defer D2H synchronization in Round 0 by splitting the per-AIR loop into a GPU launch phase and a CPU processing phase, eliminating ~1,246 blocking sync points.\n\n**Result**: success — STARK excl trace APC 300: -104ms vs baseline (2478ms -> 2374ms, 1.04x lower); Round 0 APC 300: -94ms vs baseline (663ms -> 569ms, 1.17x lower)\n\n**Summary**: Restructured `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` to launch all GPU kernels before processing any results. The improvement scales with APC count as expected (APC 0: negligible, APC 100: -86ms Round 0, APC 300: -93ms Round 0). The absolute savings were lower than the plan's estimate of 200-300ms, likely because CPU-side work in the logup kernel setup already provided partial GPU overlap in the original code. The optimization establishes a clean two-phase structure that enables future rayon parallelization of Phase 2 CPU work.\n\n## 2026-04-10-2145-batch-degenerate-stacked-reduction\n\n**Idea**: Batch all degenerate-window kernel launches in Stacked Reduction into a single GPU kernel launch per round, eliminating ~10,348 tiny kernel launches at APC 300.\n\n**Result**: success — STARK excl trace APC 300: -283ms vs baseline (2478ms -> 2195ms, 1.13x lower); Stacked Reduction APC 300: -161ms vs baseline (308ms -> 147ms, 2.10x lower) (vs previous: -165ms, 312ms -> 147ms, 2.12x lower)\n\n**Summary**: Added a batched CUDA kernel where each block handles one degenerate window, all atomically accumulating into a shared output buffer. This replaced ~10,348 individual kernel launches (each with fill_zero + H2D + kernel + D2H sync) with a single launch cycle. The STARK excl trace APC 300/APC 0 scaling ratio improved from 1.153 (baseline) to 1.022, achieving near-parity. Combined with the previous Round 0 optimization, cumulative STARK excl trace improvement at APC 300 is 283ms (11.4%).\n\n","numLines":19,"startLine":1,"totalLines":19}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012W8TLc6bokZBFd3j9QyVQP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the correct numbers:\n\nAfter commit 6fccffce (the target state):\n- **Round 0**: APC 0 = 175ms, APC 300 = 569ms\n- **STARK excl trace**: APC 0 = 2146ms, APC 300 = 2195ms (from the stacked reduction report)\n\nWait, the d2h report shows STARK excl trace APC 300 = 2374ms after commit 69fc59ca. Then the stacked reduction report says STARK excl trace APC 300 = 2195ms after commit 6fccffce. Let me verify this from the log.\n\n\nLooking at the log entries, the d2h-sync commit reduced STARK excl trace APC 300 from 2478ms to 2374ms, and then stacked-reduction brought it down further to 2195ms—a total reduction of 283ms from baseline. So at the final commit, STARK excl trace sits at APC 0 ≈ 2146ms and APC 300 = 2195ms, giving a ratio of about 1.022.\n\nFor Round 0, the d2h report shows APC 300 = 569ms after the sync, and since stacked-reduction only touched the stacked reduction logic itself, Round 0 should remain at 569ms. The reviewer's concern is that the CPU overhead in Round 0 is roughly 569ms minus the 479ms GPU kernel time from nsight, which gives only ~90ms of headroom—but the plan claims 100-150ms in savings, which would exceed what's actually available. I need to check whether that 479ms baseline is still valid after d2h-sync, since the numbers may have shifted. The key insight is that nsight measures individual kernel durations summed together (479ms), not wall-clock time from first to last kernel launch. The actual wall-clock GPU time could be larger due to gaps between kernels caused by CPU setup overhead, and eliminating that overhead would reduce those gaps and the effective GPU time accordingly.\n\nWorking through the math with 370 AIRs where CPU setup takes ~0.2ms and GPU kernels run ~0.3ms on average, the total time depends heavily on how much CPU work overlaps with GPU execution. The d2h-sync optimization already improved overlap by deferring data transfers, but the remaining 90ms of CPU overhead suggests there's still non-overlapped serialization happening. The reviewer's point that we can't save more than 90ms from CPU elimination seems reasonable, which makes claiming 100-150ms in savings feel too optimistic.\n\nA more grounded estimate would be 40-70ms from Part 1 by reducing but not fully eliminating CPU overhead—some weight computation and host-to-device transfers will still be necessary—plus another 30-50ms from Part 2's parallelization gains. Let me check the stacked reduction report at that commit to see the actual breakdown.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3238,"cache_read_input_tokens":106285,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3238},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7092ff3-dad3-4115-bd08-06aa847648b3","uuid":"9c6839de-a7dc-42cb-8063-6a58c072f618"}
