{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017ogjbmKNaLZfWZyxEsaWYb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Task: 2026-04-11-0945-precompute-round0-logup-rules\n2\t\n3\tTask name: 2026-04-11-0945-precompute-round0-logup-rules\n4\t\n5\t## Problem Statement\n6\t\n7\tRound 0 constraint evaluation at APC 300 takes ~569ms — the single largest scaling bottleneck. While APC 300 has 2.15x fewer constraint instances than APC 0 (505M vs 1.13B), Round 0 at APC 300 is 3.25x SLOWER than APC 0 (569ms vs 175ms), contributing +394ms of inverse scaling.\n8\t\n9\tThe root cause: for each of the ~370 AIR instances with interactions (per segment, 2 segments), the Round 0 logup code path rebuilds the symbolic expression DAG and compiles GPU evaluation rules from scratch. This CPU-intensive work depends only on the AIR's constraint structure (fixed at keygen time) but is recomputed on every `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` call.\n10\t\n11\tProfiling data shows Round 0 GPU kernel time is ~479ms at APC 300 (baseline nsight), while total Round 0 time is 569ms post-d2h-sync. The ~90ms gap is CPU overhead: DAG construction, rule compilation, weight computation, and H2D transfers. However, the actual CPU overhead is larger because the pipeline masks some of it — GPU utilization drops when per-AIR CPU setup (0.3-0.6ms) exceeds per-AIR GPU kernel time (0.2-0.4ms for small AIRs).\n12\t\n13\tCompounding the issue: when batching is disabled (as for the pairing benchmark with large `skip_domain`), each AIR's DAG and rules are built TWICE:\n14\t1. In `mod.rs` Phase 1 loop (lines 907-926) — computing rules to check batch eligibility\n15\t2. In `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` (round0.rs lines 219-265) — rebuilding the same DAG and rules for the actual kernel launch\n16\t\n17\tThe Phase 1 computation is completely wasted for non-batch-eligible AIRs. This duplicate work adds ~0.2ms per AIR × 370 AIRs × 2 segments = ~148ms of pure waste.\n18\t\n19\tAdditionally, the Phase 2 IDFT processing loop (mod.rs lines 1184-1281) processes all 623 AIR results sequentially. Each iteration performs transpose + IDFT operations that are CPU-bound and embarrassingly parallel (each AIR writes to independent `batch_sp_poly` indices).\n20\t\n21\t**Note**: The batch-round0-coset-parallel commit (74134b4e, most recent) appears to cause a severe performance regression — the pairing benchmark fails to complete within 2 minutes. This commit should be investigated and likely reverted before applying this optimization. The measurements in this task reference the post-stacked-reduction state (commit 6fccffce).\n22\t\n23\t## Proposed Approach\n24\t\n25\t### Part 1: Pre-compute Round 0 logup interaction rules at keygen time\n26\t\n27\tIn `AirDataGpu::new()` (pkey.rs), pre-compile the Round 0 logup interaction rules alongside the existing `interaction_rules`:\n28\t\n29\t1. **Add `round0_interaction_rules` to `AirDataGpu`**: Build the interaction DAG with `buffer_vars=true` (as the Round 0 kernel requires) and compile rules via `SymbolicRulesGpu::new(&dag, true)`. Store the compiled rules, encoded rules on device, and a mapping from interaction index → rule index for fast weight computation.\n30\t\n31\t2. **Refactor `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu`** (round0.rs): Accept pre-compiled rules as a parameter instead of rebuilding from scratch. Remove the internal DAG construction and rule compilation (lines 219-265). Only compute runtime-dependent weights (numer_weights, denom_weights) using the pre-computed index mapping.\n32\t\n33\t3. **Simplify Phase 1 loop in `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`** (mod.rs): When `batching_enabled` is false, skip the DAG/rule construction entirely (lines 907-926) and go directly to `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` with pre-compiled rules. When batching IS enabled, use the pre-compiled rules for batch metadata instead of recompiling.\n34\t\n35\t### Part 2: Parallelize Phase 2 IDFT processing\n36\t\n37\tConvert the sequential Phase 2 loop (mod.rs lines 1184-1281) to use `rayon::par_iter()`:\n38\t\n39\t1. **Pre-fetch all D2H data** before entering the parallel section. For non-batched AIRs that still have `DeviceBuffer` results, call `to_host()` before the parallel loop (D2H transfers need the CUDA context).\n40\t\n41\t2. **Parallel IDFT processing**: Use `pending.into_par_iter()` to process all AIR results concurrently. Each iteration writes to independent indices in `batch_sp_poly` (via `entry.trace_idx`), so no synchronization is needed beyond the atomic slice access.\n42\t\n43\t## Expected Outcome\n44\t\n45\t**Round 0 improvement (APC 300)**:\n46\t- Eliminating duplicate DAG/rule compilation: ~100-150ms savings\n47\t  - Phase 1 loop waste (mod.rs): ~74ms/segment × 2 = 148ms\n48\t  - Round 0 function compilation (round0.rs): savings partially masked by GPU pipeline\n49\t- Net Round 0 reduction: ~100-150ms (from ~569ms to ~419-469ms)\n50\t- At APC 0 (99 AIR instances): minimal savings (~5-15ms) — optimization primarily helps APC 300\n51\t\n52\t**Phase 2 IDFT parallelization (APC 300)**:\n53\t- Sequential IDFT for 623 AIRs → parallel on 8+ cores\n54\t- Expected savings: 30-50ms\n55\t\n56\t**Combined STARK excl trace improvement (APC 300)**:\n57\t- Total: ~130-200ms reduction (from ~2195ms to ~1995-2065ms)\n58\t- Scaling ratio: from ~1.014 to ~0.95-0.97 (APC 300 becomes meaningfully faster than APC 0)\n59\t\n60\t## Success Criteria\n61\t\n62\t1. **Primary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 decreases by ≥100ms vs pre-optimization baseline (2195ms → ≤2095ms)\n63\t2. **Secondary**: Round 0 APC 300 decreases by ≥80ms (569ms → ≤489ms)\n64\t3. **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 ratio for STARK excl trace ≤ 0.98 (APC 300 measurably faster)\n65\t4. **Correctness**: All 94 openvm-cuda-backend tests pass; proof verification succeeds for APC 0/100/300\n66\t5. **No regression at APC 0**: STARK excl trace APC 0 change ≤ ±20ms\n67\t\n68\t## Considered Alternatives\n69\t\n70\t### 1. Batch GKR input evaluation kernels\n71\t- **Idea**: Batch the per-AIR `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` launches (791 instances at APC 300) into fewer large launches, similar to the Round 0 batching approach.\n72\t- **Expected impact**: ~200-300ms (nsight data from 003 directory shows 5x GPU time reduction: 428ms → 86ms)\n73\t- **Why not chosen**: Complex implementation requiring new CUDA kernels + Rust orchestration. Same memory constraint challenges as batch-round0 (which itself appears to cause a regression). The GKR input eval already uses pre-compiled rules from the proving key, so the redundant compilation savings don't apply here. Higher risk for uncertain return — the 003 directory metrics show STARK excl trace barely improved despite the GPU time reduction, suggesting hidden CPU overhead offsets.\n74\t\n75\t### 2. Multi-stream Round 0 pipelining\n76\t- **Idea**: Use 2-4 CUDA streams to overlap GPU kernel execution with CPU Phase 2 IDFT processing.\n77\t- **Expected impact**: 50-100ms savings\n78\t- **Why not chosen**: Complex synchronization logic, risk of race conditions with VPMM memory management. The Phase 2 IDFT parallelization achieves similar savings with much less complexity (rayon vs CUDA stream management).\n79\t\n80\t### 3. Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels\n81\t- **Idea**: Similar to the degenerate stacked reduction batching, batch the 7,467 per-window non-degenerate kernel launches.\n82\t- **Expected impact**: ~50-80ms on Stacked Reduction (from 147ms)\n83\t- **Why not chosen**: Lower absolute impact than Round 0 optimization. Stacked Reduction already improved 2x. Diminishing returns.\n84\t\n85\t### 4. Rayon-only Phase 2 IDFT (without rule pre-computation)\n86\t- **Idea**: Just parallelize the Phase 2 loop without addressing the redundant compilation.\n87\t- **Expected impact**: 30-50ms only\n88\t- **Why not chosen**: Included as Part 2 of the chosen approach. On its own, the savings are too small to justify a full optimization iteration. Combining with Part 1 gives meaningful total impact.\n89\t\n90\t### 5. Investigate and fix batch-round0 regression\n91\t- **Idea**: Debug why commit 74134b4e causes the pairing benchmark to hang.\n92\t- **Why not chosen as primary**: This is a bug fix/investigation, not a new optimization. Should be done as a prerequisite step during the Execute phase (likely a revert), not as the optimization itself.\n93\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011psM59SqEkyPa9tLB1Y2jQ","session_id":"b7092ff3-dad3-4115-bd08-06aa847648b3","uuid":"a411748e-5bb8-44b6-b677-8dc7dc201304","timestamp":"2026-04-11T07:57:46.592Z"}
